ated.\nL119: \nL120: * * *\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[1710.09745] Pushing Memory Bandwidth Limitations Through Efficient Implementations of Block-Krylov Space Solvers on GPUs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09745)\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09745\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 168\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # High Energy Physics - Lattice\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Oct 2017 (【5†v1】), last revised 7 Aug 2018 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:Pushing Memory Bandwidth Limitations Through Efficient Implementations of Block-Krylov Space Solvers on GPUs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†M. A. Clark】, 【7†Alexei Strelchenko】, 【8†Alejandro Vaquero】, 【9†Mathias Wagner】, 【10†Evan Weinberg】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Pushing Memory Bandwidth Limitations Through Efficient Implementations of Block-Krylov Space Solvers on GPUs, by M. A. Clark and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【11†View PDF】 【12†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Lattice quantum chromodynamics simulations in nuclear physics have benefited from a tremendous number of algorithmic advances such as multigrid and eigenvector deflation. These improve the time to solution but do not alleviate the intrinsic memory-bandwidth constraints of the matrix-vector operation dominating iterative solvers. Batching this operation for multiple vectors and exploiting cache and register blocking can yield a super-linear speed up.\nL17: Block-Krylov solvers can naturally take advantage of such batched matrix-vector operations, further reducing the iterations to solution by sharing the Krylov space between solves. However, practical implementations typically suffer from the quadratic scaling in the number of vector-vector operations. Using the QUDA library, we present an implementation of a block-CG solver on NVIDIA GPUs which reduces the memory-bandwidth complexity of vector-vector operations from quadratic to linear.\nL18: We present results for the HISQ discretization, showing a 5x speedup compared to highly-optimized independent Krylov solves on NVIDIA's SaturnV cluster.\nL19: Comments:  | 15 pages, 14 figures, in press\nL20: Subjects:  | High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat); Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)\nL21: Report number:  | FERMILAB-PUB-17-592-CD\nL22: Cite as:  | 【13†arXiv:1710.09745】 [hep-lat]\nL23:    | (or 【14†arXiv:1710.09745v2】 [hep-lat] for this version)\nL24:    | 【15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1710.09745†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Journal reference:  | Comp. Phys. Comm. 2018\nL26: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【16†https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2018.06.019†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Alejandro Marino Vaquero Avilés-Casco [【17†view email】]\nL30: 【5†[v1]】 Thu, 26 Oct 2017 15:04:25 UTC (297 KB)\nL31: [v2] Tue, 7 Aug 2018 19:13:50 UTC (565 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Pushing Memory Bandwidth Limitations Through Efficient Implementations of Block-Krylov Space Solvers on GPUs, by M. A. Clark and 3 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * 【11†View PDF】\nL40:   * 【12†HTML (experimental)】\nL41:   * 【18†TeX Source】\nL42: \nL43: 【19†view license】\nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: hep-lat\nL47: \nL48: 【20†< prev】   |   【21†next >】\nL49: \nL50: 【22†new】 | 【23†recent】 | 【24†2017-10】\nL51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: 【25†physics】\nL55: 【26†physics.comp-ph】\nL56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * 【27†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.net】\nL60:   * 【28†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL